%[text] # Task 2: Inspect, Trace, and Verify Requirements
%[text] This live script walks through Task 2 of Exercise 2 entirely from the command line, so you can follow along without clicking through the Simulink canvas and the Requirements Editor.
%[text] You will **inspect the requirements**, trace each one to **where it is implemented in the model**, and then run the automated tests to see **whether each requirement is satisfied**.
%%
%[text] ## Background: 
%[text] The requirements reflect the behaviors we explored in Part 1. The **signal-processing requirements (SP)** cover functions such as DC blocking, the eight frequency channels, envelope extraction, and denoising. The **electrical-stimulation requirements (ST)** cover behaviors such as charge-balanced pulses, interleaved stimulation, and using pulse amplitude to represent loudness.
%[text] **The key idea is that these behaviors are not just captured in the model. Each one is explicitly specified as a requirement, traced to its implementation, and connected to a test that verifies it.**
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:4fe6)
%%
%[text] ## Setup
%[text] Load the model so its implementation links resolve, then load the requirement set.
model = "CochlearImplantModel";
load_system(model)
rs = slreq.load("CochlearImplantRequirements");
slreq.load("tCochlearImplantRequirements.m");
%%
%[text] ## 1. Inspect the Requirements
%[text] List every functional requirement with its ID, summary, and description. These requirements fall into two groups: **SP** (signal processing) and **ST** (electrical stimulation).
%[text] Notice INFO-01 in the list. It is an informational requirement that records background rationale rather than a testable behavior, so it has no implementation or test and is expected to stay empty in the status columns.
requirements = functionalRequirements(rs);
inspectTable = table(string({requirements.Id}'), string({requirements.Summary}'),string({requirements.Description}'),... %[output:group:65b6d11c] %[output:78579b26]
    VariableNames=["Id" "Summary" "Description"]) %[output:group:65b6d11c] %[output:78579b26]
%%
%[text] ## 2. Trace to Implementation
%[text]  Each requirement is linked to the block or subsystem in CochlearImplantModel that realizes it. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:042e)
%[text] Updating the implementation status refreshes those links, and the table below shows, requirement by requirement, exactly where each behavior lives in the design.
rs.updateImplementationStatus();
implementedBy = implementingBlocks(requirements);
implTable = table(string({requirements.Id}'), string({requirements.Summary}'), implementedBy, ... %[output:group:9420b5c0] %[output:9a6bb9c3]
    VariableNames=["Id" "Summary" "ImplementedBy"]) %[output:group:9420b5c0] %[output:9a6bb9c3]
%[text]  For a clickable version, we print each link to the output. **Click a block name to open the model and select that block.**
printImplementationLinks(requirements) %[output:3e65e1fa]
%%
%[text] ## 3. Verify the Requirements
%[text] Tracing shows where a requirement is implemented, but not whether that implementation actually satisfies it — that is what verification establishes. Each requirement is linked to an automated test that drives the real model and checks one specific behavior.
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:4638)
%[text] Run the automated test suite. Each test method drives the real model and checks one requirement.
results = runtests("tCochlearImplantRequirements"); %[output:38f9699b]
%[text] Each result maps back to the requirement it verifies. A Passed result means the implementation satisfies that requirement; INFO-01 is informational, with no behavior to test, so it shows No test. A **Passed** result means the implementation satisfies that requirement.
status = verificationStatus(requirements, results);
verifyTable = table(string({requirements.Id}'), string({requirements.Summary}'), status, ... %[output:group:3b2eb47f] %[output:6eeb32f6]
    VariableNames=["Id" "Summary" "Verification"]) %[output:group:3b2eb47f] %[output:6eeb32f6]
%[text] As with the implementation links, print a *clickable* link to the test that verifies each requirement. **Click a test name to open the test file at that method.**
printVerificationLinks(requirements) %[output:235d8152]
%%
%[text] ## Summary
%[text] This gives us **end-to-end traceability**, connecting each requirement to its implementation and the test evidence that verifies it.
summaryTable = table(string({requirements.Id}'), string({requirements.Summary}'), implementedBy, status, ... %[output:group:55ce2c8d] %[output:96283385]
    VariableNames=["Id" "Summary" "ImplementedBy" "Verification"]) %[output:group:55ce2c8d] %[output:96283385]
%%
%[text] ## Clean up
%[text] Unload the requirement set and close the model.
slreq.clear
close_system(model, 0)

